Project: MW–M31 Density Analysis Pipeline (Diagnostics, Model Fitting & Visualization Expansion)
Hours Worked: 10.5

Work Summary:
Continued large-scale development of the MW–M31 merger density analysis framework by implementing advanced diagnostics, analytical profile fitting, spatiotemporal residual analysis, synchronized visualization systems, and publication-quality summary products. Expanded the density pipeline into a comprehensive structural evolution toolkit capable of tracking progenitor mixing, evaluating theoretical halo models, visualizing density evolution through time, and generating automated research outputs.

(Started with a Claude generated scaffold)

Major accomplishments included:

• Designed Diagnostic 5 to compare Milky Way and Andromeda radial density distributions across multiple evolutionary epochs. Implemented side-by-side progenitor density profiles together with radial density ratio diagnostics to quantify local dominance, identify mixing interfaces, and monitor survival of progenitor cores throughout the merger sequence.

• Developed Diagnostic 6 introducing a mass-mixing fraction formalism
      f_mix(r,t)=min(ρ_MW,ρ_M31)/ρ_total
  to measure local spatial overlap between progenitor material. Generated interpolated spatiotemporal heatmaps tracking outward migration of the merger mixing front while computing time-averaged mixing profiles to characterize long-term structural homogenization.

• Implemented complete analytical halo profile fitting framework supporting multiple theoretical density models including Navarro–Frenk–White (NFW), Einasto, and Hernquist profiles. Constructed nonlinear least-squares optimization routines operating in logarithmic density space, with automatic parameter initialization, constrained optimization, reduced χ² evaluation, and robust convergence handling.

• Developed reusable fitting engine capable of estimating characteristic halo parameters including NFW scale radius, Einasto shape parameter α, Hernquist scale length, and corresponding goodness-of-fit metrics for every sampled simulation epoch.

• Generated multi-model comparison figures overlaying measured density profiles with optimized analytical solutions at representative merger stages, allowing direct assessment of theoretical halo model performance during the interaction.

• Implemented Diagnostic 8 to evaluate spatial fitting accuracy through time by computing normalized NFW residual fields
      Δ(r,t)=(ρ_measured−ρ_NFW)/ρ_NFW
  and visualizing these residuals as spatiotemporal heatmaps. Added time-averaged residual diagnostics to identify persistent deviations associated with post-merger cores, tidal debris, and outer-envelope stripping.

• Developed Diagnostic 9 for long-term structural telemetry of fitted parameters. Automated chronological tracking of half-mass radii, NFW scale radius evolution, Einasto shape evolution, and comparative reduced χ² histories, enabling quantitative evaluation of structural evolution throughout the merger.

• Constructed synchronized density profile animation engine displaying evolving total density distributions, progenitor-separated radial profiles, and logarithmic density slope evolution within a unified multi-panel visualization. Added historical ghost-profile overlays to emphasize temporal trajectory and improve visualization of structural evolution.

• Developed dynamic 2D surface density morphology animation framework for projected stellar mass distributions. Implemented logarithmic smoothing, adaptive normalization, synchronized progenitor decomposition, and chronological rendering of total, Milky Way, and Andromeda surface density maps.

• Designed comprehensive publication-ready master summary dashboard integrating the complete density analysis workflow into a single multi-panel figure. Combined radial density evolution, projected profiles, gradient heatmaps, mixing diagnostics, analytical model performance, structural parameter evolution, and representative morphology snapshots into a unified overview suitable for research presentations and manuscript preparation.

• Expanded visualization framework with consistent dark-theme styling, adaptive logarithmic scaling, synchronized color mapping, improved annotation systems, interpolation-based heatmap construction, standardized labeling, and automated figure export routines across all diagnostics.

• Implemented automated pipeline cleanup procedures, temporary filesystem removal, output manifest generation, storage accounting, and execution summaries documenting generated figures, animations, and total output volume for reproducible computational workflows.

Overall Progress:
Substantially extended the MW–M31 density analysis pipeline beyond basic radial profile measurements into a comprehensive structural evolution framework incorporating theoretical halo model fitting, merger mixing diagnostics, residual topology analysis, parameter chronology, synchronized animations, and publication-quality visualization products. The pipeline now provides an end-to-end environment for quantitatively analyzing density evolution, progenitor interaction, and halo structural transformation during galaxy mergers.
